NoteWearable does not mean automatically safe



Teacher approval is required for attachment hardware and finishing materials. Remove sharp edges and fragile projections. Do not use a classroom prototype where failure, ingestion, skin sensitivity or entanglement could create a hazard.


Review Slicing, Orientation & Supports and Tolerances & Functional Parts as needed.

Test the interface
Create a small coupon containing several clearances, hole diameters, or pin/socket pairs around the target dimension. Label each value in the model. Print with the intended printer, material, orientation, and profile; measure the result and choose a fit from evidence.
Functional choices include:

free-running clearance for moving or removable parts;
sliding fit for guided assembly;
snug fit for repeatable location;
press fit where material behaviour and removal requirements make it appropriate;
pilot, clearance, counterbore, or captured-nut features for conventional fasteners.

First task
Draw front, top and side views. Mark:

plaque size and thickness;
support footprint;
slot or fastener;
likely tipping direction;
one dimension that needs a test.

A good stand

stays upright after a light desk bump;
keeps centre of mass over its support area;
does not hide the engraving;
assembles without forcing parts;
records what changed after the cardboard or scrap test.

First task
Measure three examples of the object. Record:

widest controlling dimension;
range of measurements;
removal clearance;
centre-to-centre spacing;
remaining material at the edge and between openings.

Ready to fabricate?

measured bottle parameters replaced defaults;
BOM matches visible parts;
assembly order is possible;
fit coupon passed;
free-standing stability or wall plan is approved;
file still says unverified until the physical record exists.

Evidence, not decoration
Hand in:

paper planning sheet;
editable SVG master;
fabrication/export file;
BOM or part list;
fit/stability evidence;
one clear revision statement.

Prepare the design

simplify the SVG and remove accidental overlaps, stray nodes and duplicate lines;
set the document units and intended physical scale;
check antlers, points, bridges and other small features against the actual material/process;
make the hanging hole large enough for the approved string or hook and leave enough material around it;
separate CUT geometry from ENGRAVE artwork using the local file convention;
add original personalization without weakening critical regions;
print at full size or cut a small approved test before producing the final ornament.

1. Plan before opening Photopea
Write down the name you want people to read. Then make three small paper sketches. Change at least one important idea in each sketch:

the size of the logo;
the size or position of the name;
the font;
the amount of empty space; or
the relationship between the logo and the first letter.

Download the full-size 11 × 4 inch paper prototype sheet
Print at Actual size / 100%. Do not choose Fit. Hold each idea about 2 metres away and circle the one that communicates your name fastest.

What you will make
Make a 5–8 second animated intro using the logo you already designed.
Your animation needs:

at least two intentional changes, such as move, scale, rotate, fade, or reveal;
a clear beginning, middle, and ending;
a readable moment when the finished logo holds still;
motion that matches one feeling; and
one visible improvement after testing or feedback.

This example reveals each part, holds the complete logo, and fades out.

Before you start
Finish Lesson 2A: Warm-Up: Trace the AA Logo first.
Open Photopea in a browser. Download the rough landscape sketch, or use a clear photo of your own sketch.
You will use:

the Layers panel;
the Ellipse Tool;
the Pen Tool in Shape mode;
the Move Tool; and
the colour picker.

6. Fix the size and spacing
Use the Move Tool to move a whole shape. Use the Direct Select Tool to move one point.
Check that:

the two peaks look balanced;
the centre valley is clear;
the sun does not cover a peak;
the river follows the mountain base; and
no part is too close to the edge.

Make small changes. Look at the whole logo after each change.

Check your work
Before you submit, check that:

the PSD has four separate shape layers;
every layer has a clear name;
the reference is hidden and locked;
the logo still works when the reference is hidden;
the SVG contains only the finished artwork; and
each part can be selected separately.

Accessible design
Accessible design helps more people see and understand your work. Before exporting, use the complete Accessible Design guide to check the font at different zoom levels, contrast, open letter shapes, colour choices, and alt text.

Check your work
Continue when:

the canvas is 11 × 4 inches;
the background is transparent;
the left 4 × 4 inch area is empty;
the name is easy to read;
the letters have strong contrast and open shapes;
the name is centred from top to bottom; and
there is a good margin on the right.

What you will make
You will rebuild the hand-drawn AA mark with four clean vector lines:

a left diagonal;
a right diagonal;
a vertical centre line; and
a horizontal crossbar.

3. Draw the other three lines
Draw the other diagonal, the centre line, and the crossbar. Keep each line on its own shape layer.



Four vector lines rebuild the AA mark.


Check that:

the two outside lines meet at one top point;
the lower ends are at the same height;
the centre line is straight;
the crossbar is level; and
the four lines have the same thickness.

Check your work
Continue when:

the four lines are editable shape layers;
both sides look even;
the line thickness matches;
the photograph is hidden; and
the SVG contains only the finished mark.

Why SVG matters
SVG is a common vector file type. It can store the page size, paths, groups, fills, and lines. It can move between many programs and gives fabrication software geometry it can turn into machine paths. Always check:

document units are millimetres when physical size matters;
a known rectangle measures correctly after export and import;
text is available in the destination environment or converted to paths in a copy;
the software distinguishes filled artwork from vector paths;
repeated or overlapping cut lines have been removed.

Beginner tool: Pencil2D
Pencil2D is a free tool for simple raster and vector animation. Keep the first task short:

storyboard a start, change and return;
create the main keyframes first;
turn on onion skinning and add only the in-between frames needed;
preview the loop at different timings;
hold important poses longer instead of redrawing them repeatedly;
export using the teacher-approved format and reopen the export.

Design the appearance of the joint
The joint can be hidden or become part of the visual language. Consider:

repetition and rhythm in fingers, slots or layers;
symmetry or intentional asymmetry;
grain direction across connected parts;
contrast between a clean face and a dark laser-cut edge;
consistent corner radii and edge treatment;
whether keys, fasteners and laminations should be displayed or concealed.

Aesthetic choices should reinforce the object’s function rather than disguise awkward fit or weak structure.

5. Build evidence before the full product
Create the smallest useful test first. This might be one opening, slot series, hook, magnet pocket, label system, removable joint, or cardboard reach mock-up.
Test and record:

fit and clearance using the actual tools;
removal and return time;
one-hand access and neighbouring-tool collisions;
visibility and missing-tool recognition;
stability, mounting, and expected load direction;
assembly time, part count, material use, and estimated cost;
user words, actions, and emotional response;
one change made because of the evidence.

Do not claim a safe load, accessibility, durability, or ergonomic success without a suitable test.

6. Submit the design story
Your submission must include:

a precedent board with at least three attributed references;
a feature-and-problem analysis in your own words;
a measured improvement brief with criteria and constraints;
three concepts and a decision matrix;
your own editable CAD or vector source and an exported fabrication file;
a critical-dimension coupon or low-fidelity prototype;
build photographs and a short user test;
a before-and-after comparison against the chosen precedent;
revision notes showing what changed and why;
a final evaluation that distinguishes measured results from opinions and unverified claims.

Required evidence

situation, user, need, requirements, and restrictions;
measurements and the source of critical dimensions;
alternatives and justification for using 3D printing;
editable CAD model and exported manufacturing file;
orientation and slicer decisions with a toolpath preview;
a fit or feature test where uncertainty is high;
observations from realistic use;
at least one model revision tied to evidence.

What a later revision could improve

add labels or silhouettes so every tool has an obvious home;
test and document the one-sided French-cleat option;
add a positive carry-retention feature if the organizer will be moved while loaded;
test one-handed removal with several users;
record a tip test while removing an outside knife;
measure and document the minimum neighbouring-handle clearance.

The useful strengths of this build should remain visible in the next concept. A later design does not need to change everything.

Start with clear letter shapes




A serif capital H beside a sans serif capital H. Small finishing strokes are marked on the serif letter and shown removed from the sans serif letter.




The guide uses this comparison to introduce serifs. Source: Typogram.

Sans serif is a useful starting point for small labels and screen text, but category alone does not make a font accessible. A familiar serif can outperform a thin, condensed, or decorative sans serif. Compare the actual letters at the actual size.
Look for:

open spaces inside letters such as a, e, B, and 8;
clear differences between I, l, 1, O, and 0;
enough line thickness for the screen, printer, cutter, or engraver;
comfortable spacing between letters, words, and lines;
a size that still works after you step back.




Four versions of Andrade compare serif, sans serif, rounded display, and script fonts.

A simple student source record
For every external reference, save:

creator or organization;
page, product, project, or file title;
direct URL and access date;
access label and stated licence;
what you learned from it;
what you reused, adapted, or deliberately changed;
what you verified through measurement, testing, or another source.

Do not submit a folder of links as research. Turn sources into evidence, criteria, alternatives, decisions, tests, and an original result.

Worked student models
Question 1 is the complete example. Questions 2 and 4 are additional models. Complete Questions 3 and 5 independently.






NoteQuestion 2: GUESS solution





Given: \(V_T=18.0\,\mathrm{V}\), \(R_1=3.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}\), \(R_2=6.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}\), \(R_3=3.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}\).
Unknowns: \(R_{23}\), \(R_T\), \(I_T\), \(V_1\), \(V_{23}\), \(I_2\), \(I_3\), \(P_T\).
General equations: use Equations Equation 7, Equation 3, Equation 8, Equation 5, and Equation 9.
Substitute and solve
\[
R_{23}=\frac{(6.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega})(3.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega})}
{6.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}+3.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}}
=2.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}
\tag{27}\]
\[
R_T=3.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}+2.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}
=5.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}
\tag{28}\]
\[
I_T=\frac{18.0\,\mathrm{V}}{5.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}}
=3.60\,\mathrm{mA}
\tag{29}\]
\[
V_1=(3.60\,\mathrm{mA})(3.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega})
=10.8\,\mathrm{V}
\tag{30}\]
\[
V_{23}=18.0\,\mathrm{V}-10.8\,\mathrm{V}
=7.2\,\mathrm{V}
\tag{31}\]
\[
I_2=\frac{7.2\,\mathrm{V}}{6.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}}
=1.20\,\mathrm{mA},\qquad
I_3=\frac{7.2\,\mathrm{V}}{3.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}}
=2.40\,\mathrm{mA}
\tag{32}\]
\[
P_T=(18.0\,\mathrm{V})(3.60\,\mathrm{mA})
=64.8\,\mathrm{mW}
\tag{33}\]
Statement: \(R_{23}=2.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}\), \(R_T=5.0\,\mathrm{k\Omega}\), \(I_T=3.60\,\mathrm{mA}\), \(V_1=10.8\,\mathrm{V}\), \(V_{23}=7.2\,\mathrm{V}\), \(I_2=1.20\,\mathrm{mA}\), \(I_3=2.40\,\mathrm{mA}\), and \(P_T=64.8\,\mathrm{mW}\).
Check: \(I_2+I_3=I_T\) and \(V_1+V_{23}=V_T\).









NoteQuestion 4: GUESS solution





Given: \(V_T=20.0\,\mathrm{V}\), \(R_1=1.2\,\mathrm{k\Omega}\), \(R_2=2.4\,\mathrm{k\Omega}\), \(I_T=10.0\,\mathrm{mA}\).
Unknowns: \(V_1\), \(V_{23}\), \(I_2\), \(I_3\), \(R_3\), \(R_T\).
General equations
\[
V_1=I_TR_1,\qquad V_{23}=V_T-V_1,\qquad
I_2=\frac{V_{23}}{R_2}
\tag{34}\]
\[
I_3=I_T-I_2,\qquad
R_3=\frac{V_{23}}{I_3},\qquad
R_T=\frac{V_T}{I_T}
\tag{35}\]
Substitute and solve
\[
V_1=(10.0\,\mathrm{mA})(1.2\,\mathrm{k\Omega})
=12.0\,\mathrm{V}
\tag{36}\]
\[
V_{23}=20.0\,\mathrm{V}-12.0\,\mathrm{V}
=8.0\,\mathrm{V}
\tag{37}\]
\[
I_2=\frac{8.0\,\mathrm{V}}{2.4\,\mathrm{k\Omega}}
=3.333333\ldots\,\mathrm{mA}
\tag{38}\]
\[
I_3=10.0\,\mathrm{mA}-3.333333\ldots\,\mathrm{mA}
=6.666667\ldots\,\mathrm{mA}
\tag{39}\]
\[
R_3=\frac{8.0\,\mathrm{V}}{6.666667\,\mathrm{mA}}
=1.20\,\mathrm{k\Omega}
\tag{40}\]
\[
R_T=\frac{20.0\,\mathrm{V}}{10.0\,\mathrm{mA}}
=2.00\,\mathrm{k\Omega}
\tag{41}\]
Statement: \(V_1=12.0\,\mathrm{V}\), \(V_{23}=8.0\,\mathrm{V}\), \(I_2=3.33\,\mathrm{mA}\), \(I_3=6.67\,\mathrm{mA}\), \(R_3=1.20\,\mathrm{k\Omega}\), and \(R_T=2.00\,\mathrm{k\Omega}\).
Check: \(I_2+I_3=10.0\,\mathrm{mA}=I_T\).

